NNAAMMEE
     kkeennvv — list or modify the kernel environment

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     kkeennvv [--ll | --ss] [--hhNNqq]
     kkeennvv [--qqvv] _v_a_r_i_a_b_l_e[=_v_a_l_u_e]
     kkeennvv [--qq] --uu _v_a_r_i_a_b_l_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The kkeennvv utility will list all variables in the kernel environment if in‐
     voked without arguments.

     If the --ll option is specified, then the static environment provided by
     loader(8) will be listed instead.  Similarly, the --ss option will list the
     static environment defined by the kernel config.  Both of the --ll and --ss
     options are dependent on the kernel being configured to preserve early
     kernel environments.  The default kernel configuration does not preserve
     these environments.

     If the --hh option is specified, it will limit the report to kernel probe
     hints.  If an optional _v_a_r_i_a_b_l_e name is specified, kkeennvv will only report
     that value.  If the --NN option is specified, kkeennvv will only display vari‐
     able names and not their values.  If the --uu option is specified, kkeennvv
     will delete the given environment variable.  If the environment variable
     is followed by an optional _v_a_l_u_e, kkeennvv will set the environment variable
     to this value.

     If the --qq option is set, warnings normally printed as a result of being
     unable to perform the requested operation will be suppressed.

     If the --vv option is set, the variable name will be printed out for the
     environment variable in addition to the value when kkeennvv is executed with
     a variable name.

     Variables can be added to the kernel environment using the
     _/_b_o_o_t_/_l_o_a_d_e_r_._c_o_n_f file, or also statically compiled into the kernel using
     the statement

           eennvv _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e

     in the kernel config file.  The file can contain lines of the form

           name = value # this is a comment

     where whitespace around ‘name’ and ‘=’, and everything after a ‘#’ char‐
     acter, are ignored.  Almost any printable character except ‘=’ is accept‐
     able as part of a name.  Quotes are optional and necessary only if the
     value contains whitespace.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Show kernel probe hints variable names and filter for the uart device

           $ kenv -h -N | grep uart
           hint.uart.0.at
           hint.uart.0.flags
           hint.uart.0.irq
           hint.uart.0.port
           hint.uart.1.at
           hint.uart.1.irq
           hint.uart.1.port

     Show the value of a specific variable:

           $ kenv hint.uart.1.at
           isa

     Same as above but adding the name of the variable in the report:

           $ kenv -v hint.uart.1.at
           hint.uart.1.at="isa"

     Try to delete a variable and suppress warnings if any:

           $ kenv -q -u hint.uart.1.at

     Set the value of the verbose_loading variable

           $ kenv verbose_loading="YES"
           verbose_loading="YES"

